 # Dynamic Scene and Temporal Modeling
 
-Also called: Dynamic NeRF, Time-conditioned NeRF, 4D NeRF
+Also called: Dynamic NeRF, 4D Radiance Fields, Temporal NeRF
 
 ## Overview
-Dynamic Scene and Temporal Modeling extends the static Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) assumption to handle time-varying geometry and appearance, enabling novel view synthesis for moving objects and scenes . Unlike static variants, these methods introduce temporal coordinates or deformation fields to map 3D space to radiance properties that evolve over time . This family is distinguished by its approach to modeling motion: some methods directly condition the neural network on time , others use explicit deformation fields or warping to align canonical frames , and some employ hybrid representations like particles or semantic stratification to handle complex dynamics .
+Dynamic Scene and Temporal Modeling adapts Neural Radiance Fields to handle time-varying scenes, solving the limitation of static scene representation in video sequences [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. It enables the reconstruction and novel view synthesis of dynamic content by allowing density and color fields to vary over time [14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md]. Unlike static NeRF, which assumes a fixed geometry, these methods explicitly model temporal dynamics through various mechanisms such as direct time encoding, deformation fields, particle systems, or semantic stratification [13848-gear-nerf.md][14705-nerf-with-time-parameter.md][15541-forward-warping-for-novel-view-synthesis.md][15550-dynamic-appearance-particle-neural-radiance-field.md][15551-kfd-nerf.md].
 
 ## Shared principles
-The standard NeRF architecture [Parent](3065-nerf-hyperparameters.md) serves as the baseline. Modifications for dynamic scenes generally fall into three categories [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md]:
-1.  **Input Expansion:** Adding a temporal coordinate $t$ or motion embeddings to the input concatenation .
-2.  **Output Expansion:** Adding auxiliary heads to predict deformation fields, velocities, or semantic labels, rendered using standard transmittance weights [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
-3.  **Representation Substitution:** Replacing the continuous MLP with discrete structures (voxel grids, hash tables) or explicit motion models (particles, warping fields) [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
+The standard NeRF architecture [Parent](3065-nerf-hyperparameters.md) serves as the baseline. Modifications generally fall into three categories [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md]:
+1.  **Input Expansion:** Adding new conditioning vectors (e.g., time $t$, appearance embeddings $\mathbf{z}_i$, or fluid volume $v$) to the input concatenation [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
+2.  **Output Expansion:** Adding auxiliary heads to predict values other than $\sigma$ and $\mathbf{c}$, such as semantic labels $y_{aux}$, object codes $o$, or area encoding probabilities $p$. These are rendered using the same transmittance weights $T_i$ and opacity $\alpha_i$ as color:
+    $$
+    \hat{Y}(r) = \sum_{i=1}^{N} T_i \alpha_i y_{aux}(x_i)
+    $$
+    [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
+3.  **Representation Substitution:** Replacing the continuous MLP function with discrete structures (voxel grids, hash tables) or different implicit surfaces (SDFs) [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
 
